menu "Harman Kardom board"

config HK_BOARD_DEVKIT_N8R2
    bool "Build for the ESP32-S3 N8R2 development kit"
    default n
    help
        Build for the bring-up devkit on the bench (8 MB flash, 2 MB quad PSRAM)
        instead of the N16R8 product board of ADR-0010.

        This is not a product configuration. It changes the OTA hardware revision
        from "prototype-n16r8" to "devkit-n8r2" so that a manifest published for
        one board is refused by the other, and firmware/CMakeLists.txt refuses to
        build it with application signing enabled so it can never be published.

        Leave this off unless you are flashing the devkit. Setting it by hand
        without also selecting the 8 MB flash size and the devkit partition table
        is a configure-time error, not something you discover on the bench.

config HK_ONBOARD_STATUS_LED
    bool "Mirror the status LED onto the board's own addressable LED"
    default y
    help
        The indicator ADR-0011 specifies is three PWM channels driving an
        external RGB LED. Until that LED is wired, a bare board shows no status
        at all and every state has to be read off the serial console -- which is
        true of the prototype N16R8 exactly as it was of the bring-up devkit, so
        this is no longer gated on which board is being built.

        It mirrors exactly what those PWM channels are told to do onto the single
        addressable LED most of these boards already carry. It is a mirror, not a
        second indicator: the colour comes from the same render pass, so the two
        cannot disagree about what the device is doing.

        Harmless when the board has no such LED: the RMT peripheral drives a pin
        nothing is listening to.

config HK_ONBOARD_STATUS_LED_GPIO
    int "GPIO of the board's on-board addressable LED"
    depends on HK_ONBOARD_STATUS_LED
    default 48
    help
        48 on ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 v1.1 and later, 38 on some earlier boards and
        on several third-party clones. If the LED stays dark, this is the first
        thing to check.

        It must not collide with the assignment in hk_pins; nothing enforces that
        here, because a Kconfig integer cannot see that table.

config HK_DISPLAY_SELF_TEST
    bool "Show a colour self test for three seconds at boot"
    default n
    help
        Four flat colours and a cross, before the status screen. It answers a
        question the serial log cannot: 4-wire SPI has no readback, so a panel
        that received nothing and a panel showing another firmware's leftovers
        look identical from the device's side.

        Off in a product: nobody wants their speaker to flash colours at them.

config HK_DISPLAY
    bool "Round GC9A01 display"
    default y
    help
        Compile and run the panel. Off removes the SPI bus entirely -- not a
        blank screen, no peripheral at all.

        That is the point of having the switch. The panel moves 115,200 bytes at
        80 MHz thirty times a second, which is a large periodic burst of
        switching next to an analogue audio pair, and while the audio path is
        being brought up it is the loudest thing in the room that is not the
        speaker. Turning it off makes "is the display the noise source?" a
        question the build answers rather than one the listener guesses at.

        On means the product build. Off is for bench sessions that are chasing
        something else.

config HK_BENCH_PROVISIONAL_PROFILE
    bool "Use a compiled-in provisional profile when factory_cal has none"
    default n
    help
        DANGEROUS ON A BOARD WITH DRIVERS ATTACHED, and differently dangerous
        from the other two: this one does not bypass a gate, it FEEDS it a
        profile nobody measured, so everything downstream behaves as though the
        speaker were calibrated.

        What it supplies: a crossover corner, a subsonic corner, branch gains
        and limiter ceilings, derived on a bench from exactly two measurements
        -- the two drivers' DC resistances. The tweeter's Fs was never located,
        so the corner that protects it is an argument rather than evidence.

        It exists because the alternative is not listening at all. hk_dsp
        refuses to run without a profile, correctly, and writing a real one into
        factory_cal is a manufacturing step. This is the bench shortcut, and the
        boot log says so in as many words every time it is used.

        It also makes pack_mv_now() return the profile's own reference voltage,
        so the ceiling is used exactly as written instead of being scaled by a
        pack nobody can measure.

        Off for any build that leaves the bench.

config HK_BENCH_AUDIO_WITHOUT_PROFILE
    bool "Permit audio on a board that has no driver-protection profile"
    default n
    help
        DANGEROUS ON A BOARD WITH DRIVERS ATTACHED. Leave this off unless you
        can see that nothing is connected to the audio output.

        The gate this bypasses is the one that keeps an unmeasured tweeter
        alive: without a profile from G0/G2 there is no crossover, no protective
        high-pass and no limiter ceiling, and hk_audio refuses rather than
        inventing them. On a bench board with no DAC, no amplifier and no driver
        the risk is absent, and the receiver cannot be exercised at all while
        the gate is shut.

        It exists so that exception is written down and visible in the boot
        report, rather than being obtained by accident. Until 2026-09-08 the
        gate could be defeated without anyone deciding to: writing provisioning
        credentials put a schema version into the calibration namespace, and
        that alone was read as "calibrated".

        It is not the only gate. HK_BENCH_AUDIO_WITHOUT_POWER_TELEMETRY below
        bypasses the other one, and the amplifier is only released when both
        answer yes.

config HK_BENCH_AUDIO_WITHOUT_POWER_TELEMETRY
    bool "Permit audio on a board that cannot measure its own pack"
    default n
    help
        DANGEROUS ON A BATTERY. Leave this off unless the board is running from
        USB or a current-limited bench supply and you can see that no Li-ion
        pack is connected to it.

        The evidence this one skips, named exactly: there is no ADC driver
        (firmware stage F6) and no G3/G4 thresholds, so hk_power_evaluate() is
        handed HK_POWER_MV_UNKNOWN and a NULL limits set, returns
        HK_POWER_UNKNOWN, and hk_power_audio_permitted() then refuses. That
        refusal is CORRECT and stays correct for the product: a pack nobody can
        measure is not a pack known to be fine, and driving an amplifier from
        one is how a cell gets taken below its floor with nothing to notice.

        It is a SEPARATE symbol from HK_BENCH_AUDIO_WITHOUT_PROFILE, and
        deliberately so. The two gates answer different questions -- does a
        measured driver-protection profile exist, and does the pack allow sound
        right now -- and folding them into one switch would destroy the only
        thing these symbols are for: the record of WHICH safety evidence a
        given build went without. A board on USB with a dummy load needs this
        one and not that one; a board with a good pack and no calibration needs
        that one and not this one.

        Narrow on purpose. It only applies where the state is HK_POWER_UNKNOWN,
        which is the "no telemetry exists" case it is named after. On the day
        an ADC driver lands and reports CRITICAL, SHUTDOWN, SENSOR_FAULT or
        OVERHEAT, this symbol does nothing and the amplifier stays down. It
        does not turn the power gate off; it declares that the gate has no
        input yet.

        ONE THING IT CANNOT TELL YOU. ADR-0004 says V1 does not play while
        charging, and that lock lives inside the same function. With no
        telemetry the `charging` flag is reported false because nothing can
        read it, not because the device knows it is not charging. So on a board
        that is both on this exception and on a charger, the charge lock is not
        being enforced -- it is being answered by a placeholder. That is
        survivable on USB and it is not survivable on the XL4015 charge chain
        of ADR-0009.

config HK_BENCH_TONE_INSTEAD_OF_AIRPLAY
    bool "Replace the AirPlay receiver with a locally generated test tone"
    default n
    help
        DANGEROUS ON A BOARD WITH DRIVERS ATTACHED, for exactly the reasons the
        two symbols above are: this build makes sound, and there is still no
        crossover, no protective high-pass and no limiter in front of the
        amplifier. Leave it off unless you can see what is on the speaker
        terminals.

        It is an INSTRUMENT, not a feature. On 2026-09-08 the bench board showed
        AirPlay metadata on its screen -- so the RTSP session works -- and made
        no sound, and nothing in the log could separate "the receiver is not
        delivering samples" from "the DAC or the amplifier chain is broken".
        This symbol separates them: it puts a 1 kHz sine at -30 dBFS, generated
        by hk_tone.c, onto the same three I2S pins, so the receiver is removed
        from the question entirely. A clean tone means the fault is upstream of
        the DAC; noise or silence means it is not.

        INSTEAD OF, and the words are exact. The vendored receiver takes
        I2S_NUM_0 when it starts and holds it; so does the tone. With this set,
        hk_main does not start the receiver at all -- not the callback, not the
        RTSP server, not the mDNS advertisement -- because two owners of one
        I2S channel is a worse bug than the one being diagnosed and would be
        misread as more evidence about the DAC.

        It does not open any gate. The mute lines still follow hk_audio's
        sequence and are still released only when the profile and power gates
        both say yes, which on this board means the two bench exceptions above
        must ALSO be set or the tone will be generated into an asserted mute
        and heard by nobody. hk_main says so in the log when that is the case.

        Not in sdkconfig.bench: turning this on is an operator's decision taken
        while looking at the bench, not something inherited from a file. Set it
        with `idf.py menuconfig` under "Harman Kardom board", or add
        CONFIG_HK_BENCH_TONE_INSTEAD_OF_AIRPLAY=y to a local sdkconfig fragment
        that is not committed. The source is only compiled when it is set, so a
        release image never carries it.

        HK_BENCH_SWEEP below turns this single held tone into a stepped sweep
        for measuring a driver. That is a different instrument into a different
        circuit, and its warnings are its own.

config HK_BENCH_SWEEP
    bool "Step through frequencies to measure a driver, instead of holding one tone"
    depends on HK_BENCH_TONE_INSTEAD_OF_AIRPLAY
    default n
    help
        Replaces the fixed 1 kHz tone with a stepped sine sweep, so that one
        Nova driver's impedance curve and resonance frequency (Fs) can be read
        off a multimeter. This is the measurement that closes the largest part
        of the G0 blocker in AGENTS.md.

        WHY IT MATTERS: the tweeter's Fs sets the MINIMUM SAFE high-pass corner
        -- at least twice Fs. Until it is measured, the crossover corner in
        docs/04-acoustics/measurement-and-dsp-plan.md is a conservative guess.
        The drivers came out of a Harman Kardon Nova, carry only manufacturer
        internal codes, and the woofer has an extra magnet glued to its back, so
        no published parameter applies and measurement is the only path.

        A DIFFERENT CIRCUIT, AND THIS IS THE PART TO GET RIGHT. The intended
        connection is NOT through the amplifier:

            DAC line out -> 470 ohm 1/4 W series resistor -> ONE BARE driver ->
            DAC ground, with the meter on AC volts across the driver.

        Bare means bare: if the tweeter's 10 uF C_SAFE capacitor is fitted, take
        it out of this circuit first. At 40 Hz that capacitor is about 400 ohms
        and the sweep would measure it rather than the voice coil.

        With that in place the driver dissipates tens of microwatts, and under
        half a milliwatt at a resonance peak. That is what allows the sweep to run at
        -6 dBFS -- 24 dB above the fixed tone -- and still be gentler on a
        tweeter by orders of magnitude than the fixed tone is. The level is
        chosen so the whole curve lands inside one 200 mV meter range; it is
        not a relaxed safety limit, it is the same limit applied to a circuit
        with no amplifier in it.

        THE DANGER IS THE WIRING, AND FIRMWARE CANNOT SEE IT. Left connected to
        the TPA3110, -6 dBFS through a possibly 36 dB amplifier is well past
        clipping into an unmeasured tweeter, held near its resonance for eight
        seconds at a time. Worse, the two bench exception symbols above that
        unmute the DAC are the same two that unmute the AMPLIFIER: there is no
        configuration that releases one and holds the other. Unplug the
        amplifier's input or cut its supply. The sweep prints this and then
        stays silent for ten seconds before the first step, so there is time to
        act on it.

        The full procedure -- the circuit, the meter range, the arithmetic that
        turns two readings into ohms, the expected values and the table to fill
        in -- is in docs/02-hardware/driver-measurements.md. Read it before
        flashing this, not after.

        Runs to the end on its own: about three and a half minutes, then
        silence and the mute lines back down. It needs no button and no input,
        because a run that wants a press per step is a run nobody finishes.

config HK_BENCH_SWEEP_FINE_HZ
    int "Centre a fine pass on this frequency (0: run the coarse pass)"
    depends on HK_BENCH_SWEEP
    range 0 8000
    default 0
    help
        0 runs the COARSE pass: 22 steps at 1/3 octave from 40 Hz to 4.9 kHz,
        which is wide enough to contain both the 60 mm cone's resonance and the
        25 mm dome's, and coarse enough that a person will actually finish
        writing it down.

        Set this to the frequency of the largest reading that pass produced,
        and the next run is a FINE pass instead: 17 steps at 1/12 octave across
        two thirds of an octave either side of it. The coarse pass says which
        neighbourhood Fs is in to about 13%; the fine pass says where it is to
        about 3%, which is what a high-pass corner deserves to be derived from.

        Two passes rather than one long fine sweep because a 1/12-octave sweep
        of the whole range is 122 steps and a quarter of an hour of copying
        numbers off a meter, and eighty of those steps are known in advance to
        be flat.

        Clamped to 20 Hz - 7.35 kHz by the generator regardless of what is set
        here, because outside that band the block length stops fitting the
        buffer at one end and the sample rate stops describing a sine at the
        other.

config HK_DUAL_TRANSPORT
    bool "Offer BLE and SoftAP provisioning at the same time"
    default y
    help
        ADR-0005 offered one transport at a time because ESP-IDF's provisioning
        manager takes one scheme. That is still true of the MANAGER -- but since
        ADR-0015 the SoftAP leg no longer goes through it: the captive portal
        hands credentials over with wifi_prov_mgr_configure_sta(), so it needs no
        scheme, no protocomm instance and no second manager. The singleton only
        has to serve BLE.

        So both are offered from the first second of a factory-fresh boot and the
        phone picks. The manager runs BLE; the access point and the portal are
        brought up alongside it by hk_network.

        One honest limit: WIFI_PROV_SCHEME_BLE_EVENT_HANDLER_FREE_BTDM releases
        the Bluetooth stack when the window closes, and that release cannot be
        undone without a reboot. The product statement is therefore "both at
        first boot, BLE until that window closes".

        Turn this off to fall back to one transport at a time.

config HK_FIRST_BOOT_BLE
    bool "Open BLE instead of SoftAP when the device has no credentials"
    default y
    help
        ADR-0005 sent a device with nothing stored to SoftAP, on the reasoning
        that the app-less path must always be reachable. The owner's stated
        priority is the opposite: BLE provisioning with a QR is the setup path
        they actually use, and on 2026-09-08 it was the one they went looking
        for on a new board and could not find.

        INTERIM. This contradicts an accepted decision, so the policy is not
        settled here -- it is settled by the ADR that supersedes ADR-0005/0015's
        transport rule, once it is known whether both transports can be open at
        once. What this switch does today is make BLE reachable on a board that
        has no credentials, so it can be tested at all.

        With this off, the ADR-0005 rule applies: no credentials opens SoftAP,
        and BLE waits for a button press on a configured device.

config HK_DEVKIT_WIFI_SSID
    string "Bench Wi-Fi SSID to preload (empty: do not preload)"
    depends on HK_BOARD_DEVKIT_N8R2
    default ""
    help
        A convenience for the bench, and only for the bench.

        The devkit has no button, so the 5-second press that clears credentials
        cannot be given, and joining its SoftAP from the machine driving the
        board would drop that machine off the network it is being told to join.
        This lets the board be put on a network over USB instead.

        Put the value in firmware/sdkconfig.devkit.local, which is ignored by
        git. Do not put it in sdkconfig.devkit: that file is committed, and this
        one carries somebody's actual Wi-Fi password.

        Empty means preload nothing, which is the default and the only setting
        that should ever be committed. Existing stored credentials are never
        overwritten.

config HK_DEVKIT_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "Bench Wi-Fi password to preload (empty: do not preload)"
    depends on HK_BOARD_DEVKIT_N8R2
    default ""
    help
        See HK_DEVKIT_WIFI_SSID. This is a real password: it ends up in the
        built image, which is why it is confined to the bench board and to a
        file that is never committed.

endmenu
